{\*\generator Msftedit 5.41.21.2510;}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs20 P?S/8 Binary File Internal Description\par
\par
Last edit:\tab 27-May-2019\tab cjl\par
\par
P?S/8 supports binary files in a variety of contexts, primarily as input to the multi-purpose binary utility program known as \i P?S/8\i0  \i BIN\i0 .\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 BIN also supports binary paper-tape conversion and other options.  Documentation of P?S/8 BIN is available as a separate document.\par
\par
The binary file format is known as \i Slurp\i0  binary; this term was coined by its inventor, the legendary \ul\i Richard Lary\ulnone\i0 , generally known as the best PDP-8 programmer in the history of mankind.  The term is applied as an attempt to explain how the loader works without requiring a separate buffer (as is usually the case), even when loading from certain storage devices traditionally associated with block-oriented access.  In P?S/8, each system device generally has a companion Slurp loader component, which is used by the P?S/8 BIN utility to load binary files on any particular system.  This is a customized routine designed for the same device as the system device handler normally resident in the system kernel area, but used as a temporary replacement routine for the express purpose of efficiently loading binary files.\par
\par
Note: The P?S/8 BIN program should not be confused with the name usually associated with \i DEC\i0  standard binary paper-tapes, which are generally loaded by the DEC\i  BIN\i0  paper-tape loader.  That said, P/S/8 BIN does support certain operating modes that handle DEC BIN (and DEC \i RIM\i0 ) binary paper-tapes, including the conversion of DEC BIN format paper-tapes from/to P?S/8 Slurp-format binary files.  When the terms \ul file\ulnone\i  \i0 and \ul BIN\ulnone  are used in the same sentence, this invariably refers to Slurp format binary files and the P?S/8 BIN program that loads the files.\line\line The advantages of a hardware-specific custom binary file loader include:\line\line 1) No reserved memory areas beyond the prevailing system convention (which is\par
   generally locations 07600-07777 unless a device handler extension is required).\par
   Where relevant, the system device handler extension occupies locations \ul x\ulnone 6000-\par
   \ul x\ulnone 7777 where \ul x\ulnone  is the highest available field of extended memory.\par
\par
2) The ability to load memory with the system device write-protected (where\par
   applicable).\par
\par
3) Surprisingly rapid program loading; this is often contrary to the expectations\par
   of programmers taught to believe what the Slurp loader actually accomplishes\par
   must be "impossible".\par
\par
The best example of a Slurp binary loader implementation is the one customized for the TC01/TC08 DECtape controller.\par
\par
The TC01/TC08 system device handler loads into 07600-07777 and provides (among other resources) a read/write subroutine call to access the various logical drive units (which are implemented as either TU55 or TU56 DECtape drives).  Certain memory locations are reserved for various system parameters and passed file lists used by various system programs.  The operating system can always be restarted at location 07600 (which is the universal starting address for all single-user PDP-8 operating systems).\line\line The P?S/8 BIN program is typically used to replace the contents of memory locations 07600-07745 with the Slurp binary loader; this includes a stand-alone bootstrap (starting at 07600) which can be used to reload the operating system after the program has been loaded and executed, and no longer needed in memory.\par
\par
While the Slurp loader is in memory, no block-oriented read/write services are available.  Memory locations 07746-07777 are preserved to pass the starting block and logical unit information for each Slurp binary file to be loaded, as well as the overall program starting field and address information.\page The Slurp loader performs search operations on the DECtape-based logical drive units using the passed starting block and logical unit file parameters as were originally passed to the P?S/8 BIN program (which loaded the Slurp loader over the system device handler).  When the proper starting block is located, the program is then directly loaded into memory using the novel technique of a single-word input buffer within the Slurp loader code page.\par
\par
The reason this is possible is that 12-bit words are brought into memory by the three-cycle data break mechanism; it is possible to detect individual word transfers by monitoring the associated word count (and/or current address) locations for an expected change.  Once the word count changes, it is reset for the next transfer and the latest word transferred can then be used to effect the latest binary operation (which is primarily to load the latest word into memory and increment the location counter).\par
\par
Note: By nature, most PDP-8 binary programs consist of contiguous words; this is properly exploited in both the Slurp binary file format and the DEC BIN paper-tape format; however, DEC BIN format is much more inefficient and based on an 8-bit medium that requires unpacking into 12-bit words (and includes the overhead of leader/trailer frames).\par
\par
The Slurp loader internal operations are analogous to those of the paper-tape binary loaders which use a completely different data format on paper-tape media.  All needed operations are provided in either loader; however, paper-tape loading is far slower and designed for that slow medium.  Slurp loading is nearly as efficient as block image loading, yet is random access loading much like paper-tape loading.\par
\par
In the case of the TC01/TC08-specific Slurp loader, it is necessary for each word transfer to be completed nominally in 133 microseconds; this is easily achieved since the loader programming is short and efficient.\par
\par
The loading process continues while the DECtape read operation spans multiple blocks; tape motion is continuously forward without turn-around.  Once the Slurp end-of-file indication is processed, the next passed file parameters are processed, generally requiring a new block search.  Eventually all of the passed file parameters are processed and the loading process is complete.  At this point, a starting field and starting address are used to start the program as indicated in the original execution-class command.  (This information was provided by initialization code in the P?S/8 BIN program as determined by command-line parameters provided by the user.  Additional command-line options may have been processed prior to moving the Slurp loader through memory over the system device handler.)\par
\par
All P?S/8 releases support an alternative generic Slurp binary loader that uses virtual techniques to load binary files; however, due to the virtualization overhead, the loading process will tend to take additional time to complete.  Additionally, write enable is required on the logical device unit where the P?S/8 BIN program resides as the P?S/8 BIN program includes its own virtual buffer area.\line\line Note: In the next full release of P?S/8, this implementation will likely be slightly modified to address reserved scratch blocks on any logical device unit; as a result, the overall size of the P?S/8 BIN program will be somewhat shorter.  Future releases may use the same reserved blocks for alternate purposes to be determined later.\line\line While the majority of P?S/8 configurations include a Slurp loader customized to the same hardware as the system device handler, a few system configurations force the use of the virtual Slurp binary loader:\line\line 1) The RF08 is the only DEC three-cycle data break (DMA) device that cannot support\par
   a slurp loader; this is due to the high data rate which doesn't allow sufficient\par
   time between word transfers to handle all logical sequences of the Slurp loader.\par
\par
2) The RK8E and several other single-cycle data break devices.  No method is\par
   provided to detect word transfers; the flag raises only at the end of block\par
   transfers.\page In general, all other supported devices transfer data by asynchronous read operations; this allows a device-specific Slurp loader implementation without timing considerations.\par
\par
An advantage of the virtual Slurp loader is that the system device handler is still resident after the program is loaded; however, all customized Slurp loaders support optional reloading of the system device handler (a process which admittedly appears at first glance to be both counter-intuitive and impossible).  The only restriction on this usage (for the purpose of reloading the system device handler) is lowering the normal maximum input file count of 17 to 16 total input files; this is indicated by invoking the BIN command with the \cf2\highlight3\f1\fs24 /I\cf1\highlight0\f0\fs20  command-line option switch.  As a practical matter, the lowered limit exceeds most binary loading scenarios as Slurp binary files contain nearly 3/7 of a memory field each; the restriction is thus largely moot.\par
\line While there are many additional details regarding loading options, the reader is directed to documentation of the P?S/8 BIN program (available separately) for more information; the primary purpose of this document is to describe details of the Slurp file format.\line\line Slurp format can be broken down into a series of contiguous file blocks, each independent of other blocks with the exception of the prevailing loading address and memory field in effect carried forth from the previously loaded data block.\par
\par
Note: If a single project spans more than a single binary file, all files need to be loaded in the order originally created.\line\line Each block of Slurp format binary consists of a series of seven-word groups in contiguous order.  Except for the end-of-file block, there are always 18 seven-word groups in any block; each block also contains two waste words (since the blocks are logically 128 words each).\par
\par
Note: DECtape blocks are actually 129 12-bit words each; however, the 129th word is ignored throughout P?S/8.  On the TC01/TC08 system, the word count is generally set to -128 as required; as such, the 129th word is never transferred.  The majority of PDP-8 operating systems are designed to operate in a like manner.\par
\par
Each seven-word group breaks down as follows:\line\line 1) A control word consisting of six bit pairs used to control the disposition of\par
   the data words that immediately follow.\par
\par
2) The first of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[0-1] of the control word.\par
\par
3) The second of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[2-3] of the control word.\par
\par
4) The third of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[4-5] of the control word.\par
\par
5) The fourth of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[6-7] of the control word.\par
\par
6) The fifth of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[8-9] of the control word.\par
\par
7) The sixth of six data words; the action taken regarding this word is\par
   determined by bits[10-11] of the control word.\page  Each pair of control word bits breaks down as follows:\line\line 1) 00 - The corresponding word is a data word; load this word into the memory\par
   location pointed to by the internal load pointer then increment the load\par
   pointer for the next data operation.  The loading field value in effect\par
   determines the memory field used for data loading.\par
\par
2) 01 - The end-of-file has been reached.  The Slurp loader has finished\par
   processing this file; loading continues with the contents of the next\par
   passed file (if any).\par
\par
3) 10 - The corresponding word is an origin setting. set the load pointer to\par
   the contents of this word.\par
\par
4) 11 - The corresponding word is a field setting of the form \i CDF\i0  \ul nn\ulnone  where\par
   \ul nn\ulnone  is the new loading field in bits[6-8].  This word will be stored inline\par
   (within the Slurp loader) over an existing CDF instruction; this instruction\par
   will be executed before data words are stored; as such, all data words (if any)\par
   will be loaded into field \ul nn\ulnone .\par
\par
Despite the fact that Slurp format is inherently random access (and not block-oriented), the efficiency of data loading approaches 6/7 of that of core-image loading.\line\line Note: P?S/8 system programs are stored in core-image format for the fastest possible loading speed; however, a binary program file must first be saved in core-image format before the system device handler can be used to load and execute the program.\par
\par
Practical implementations of programs that create Slurp format binary files create predictable characteristics within the file.  This allows other utilities to determine if a file might be in Slurp binary format.  Additionally, multiple files may be loaded in the same overall loading operation.  Specific validation safeguards must be present to prevent potential loading mishap.\par
\par
A short example program follows which creates a Slurp format binary output file by program assembly with P?S/8 \i PAL\i0 .\par
\par
Note: The example program output is short enough to be entirely contained in a single file block which will be illustrated below in Figure 1.\par
\par
