Figure 1: Dump of binary output file (block 0020)\fs12\par
\fs20\par
Figure 1 above shows the contents of block 0020 just after the assembly of the example file.  A description of each word is as follows:\line\line 0000) 5340 - This is the first control word in the block which indicates the\par
      disposition of the next six data words.\par
\par
0001) 0200 - All binary files automatically generate an opening *200 origin\par
      setting.  This is universally the case with all PDP-8 assemblers.\par
\par
0002) 0200 - P?S/8 PAL outputs the prevailing current origin at the start of\par
      each binary output file.  In this example, this is redundant to the\par
      previous word only because this is the first (and only) output file.  The\par
      prevailing field setting is contained within the Slurp loader; it is\par
      initialized to load into field zero by default.\page       Note: Future releases of P?S/8 PAL may also include the prevailing field\par
      setting before the prevailing origin setting.  This will allow more robust\par
      file validity checking and the elimination of specific loading order of\par
      output files created by a particular assembly.  Future releases of P?S/8 BIN\par
      will include corresponding changes when converting binary paper-tapes to\par
      P/S/8 Slurp format binary files to achieve equivalent goals.\par
\par
0003) 6271 - The source program starts with a FIELD 7 directive; as such, a\par
      corresponding CDF 70 (6271) instruction is placed in the output file. \par
\par
      Note: Most PDP-8 assemblers can place an automatic *200 after FIELD\par
      directives.  This is generally not necessary as good programming practice\par
      demands the use of an explicit new origin setting consistent with program\par
      design.  \i OS/8 PAL8\i0  and P?S/8 PAL include command-line option switches to\par
      enable this feature; by default this action is disabled to minimize the\par
      binary file size.  Binary output intended to be punched to binary paper-tapes\par
      and ultimately loaded by the DEC "self-starting" binary loader must enable\par
      this feature to avoid a serious loading error, a problem which was\par
      identified, but never remedied.\par
\par
0004) 1000 - The source program includes *1000 at this point.\par
\par
0005) 7300 - The source program includes a CLA CLL instruction.\par
\par
0006) 6203 - The source program includes a CIF CDF 00 instruction.\par
\par
0007) 0125 - This is the second (and final) control word in the block (and\par
      entire program) which indicates the disposition of the next six data\par
      words.  (Actually, this is partially moot as the end-of-file is indicated\par
      before the end of this control word; the control word is often fleshed out\par
      with additional end-of-file control bits.)\par
\par
0010) 5603 - The program includes a JMP I 0203 instruction.\line\line 0011) 7600 - The program includes the address value of 7600 used to exit the\par
      program.\par
\par
0012) xxxx - The end-of-file point has been reached.  Words 0012-0175 are ignored.\par
      Additionally, words 0176-0177 are ignored in all Slurp binary file blocks.\par
